A||||7||batboy||all rebuilt axle assemblies the same?||||1||
Z||000000||batboy||03-07-2000||10:54 AM||||I've been shopping around for a half shaft drive axle assembly and found most rebuilt ones to be in the $70 to $80 price range and brand new OEM replacement axles were $400. I ordered a rebuilt one, but got to wondering. Is the new ones any stronger for five times the price? Are any of the rebuild axles any better than the rest? I'm assuming different brand CV joints are all pretty much the same, or are they? Any insight on the matter?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||03-07-2000||11:55 AM||||A friend of mine owns an axle shop- they replace all of the wearing parts -no reason a new one would be better than a rebuilt. It would be something like buying a new drive shaft if only the universals are shot.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||03-07-2000||12:33 PM||||I put rebuilt units on mine and I am pleased so far.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||03-07-2000||02:52 PM||||I picked up my rebuilt drive axle assembly today ($70 plus $15 core at O'Reilly's), but it'll be a day or two before I get it put on, so keep responding with info or experiences. I can always take it back and get something else. I'm about tempted to replace the other side too while it's all apart. I also have to dig out that old topic where Ogre explained the best way to change those axles. I suppose it would be smart to change axle seals too.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||03-07-2000||04:18 PM||||DO THE SEALS I put reman axles in my kitcar while I had the engine out and didn't change the seals - after putting the cradle back in the car and filling the tranny I visually  found out that I should have changed the seals. Actually I just wanted to have the experience of changing them with everything in the car.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Alex4mula||03-07-2000||04:37 PM||||Axle SEALS are very important when you change/remove axle shaft. Change them to avoid leaks. Once everything is reinstalled & you realize you have a leak its very sad. Seals are cheap, easy to remove but not always easy to install. I took my tranny to a transmission shop where they had a nice tool to install it without damaging the trans housing. Charged me nothing & took them less than 5 min.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ggarsk||03-07-2000||07:31 PM||||I'd definitly change both axles. I just did both mine even thou only one needed replacing at the time. That way you'll have 2 good axles and won't have to go thru it again down the road. Do a search for drive axle replacement,that's the thread that has most all that you need to know. Good Luck!||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||03-07-2000||10:44 PM||||Yep, I decided to do both drive axles. I ordered the second one today, it'll be in tomorrow. I also ordered those fancy (and expensive) combination axle bearing/seal from the Fiero Store. Might as well do it right the first time.||||reg||1
